  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This article is showing an early but visible impact pattern: 9 citations, 9 documented downstream reuses, and 6 scite-cited statements that support its claims. The citation trail is still small, but it is moving quickly and is already being picked up by related work on index rebalancing, index inclusion effects, and index-fund trading behavior.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in <em>Financial Analysts Journal</em> in 2023, the article argues that capitalization-weighted indices tend to add stocks after strong valuation and outperformance, and remove them after weak valuation and underperformance. Its central contribution is not just the intuition that rebalancing can move prices, but the claim that post-announcement reversal creates an opportunity for investors who avoid joining the rebalancing crowd.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article\u2019s uptake is concentrated in finance-facing scholarship. OpenAlex records 9 citations, with activity beginning in the publication year and continuing through 2026. Most of the citing works are preprints, which is consistent with a fast-moving research conversation that is still evolving. The citing literature clusters around financial markets and investment strategies, corporate finance and governance, and valuation research, suggesting that the paper is being used as a reference point for broader debates about index funds, inelastic demand, and market composition.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Reuse signals reinforce that picture. The work has been reused in 9 downstream items, including 6 preprints, 2 journal articles, and 1 piece of paratext. Several of the citing or reuse items explicitly extend the same theme, such as index inclusion effects, adverse selection costs for index funds, and the market impact of rebalancing. That pattern suggests conceptual influence rather than isolated citation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Other signals point to modest but real reach beyond the citation record. Scite shows 14 citation statements, with 6 supporting statements and 8 mentioning statements, indicating that the paper is being engaged with in the literature rather than merely listed. The record also shows one web mention in a Substack post, and a WorldCat holding, but the public-discourse and teaching signals remain limited in the available data. Overall, the article appears to be an early, specialized contribution that has already entered an active research stream on index rebalancing and passive investing.</p>",
